# Intro
A star behind which an absorbing clump casts a shadow, in a box that is NOT
periodic. A star (29.7 Msun) and a spherical clump of 5 smoothing lengths
radius lie on a diagonal of a cube of gas at Z = 0.01 Zsun, with
262144 particles (1e3 atom/cm^3, 0.1 Msun, 51.8 smoothing lengths on a side).
The star is 18 smoothing lengths from the centre of the clump. The clump is
the gas of higher metallicity: its dust makes it optically thick, with an
optical depth of 10 across its diameter in the PE band (the photoelectric heating band of the radiation field). `makeIC.py` sets
that metallicity from the dust cross-section and writes the metal mass
fractions to the initial conditions, so `params.yml` reads them with
`GEARChemistry:initial_metallicity: -1`.

An optically thick clump absorbs the light that crosses it. A second run
without the clump (`clump=0`) gives the reference for the ratio map.

`InitialConditions:periodic: 0`: the light that reaches a face of the box
leaves, and no image of the star feeds the shadow. The field of the gas within
one kernel support of a face reads about two to three times higher than
further in: leave that layer out when you measure the field. The initial
conditions give the gas an internal energy of 1 (km/s)^2 per unit mass, which
`SPH:initial_temperature: 1000` replaces by that of 1000 K. The gas at the
faces then expands out of the box: about 2 % of the particles leave in the
first 4 steps.

`params.yml` fixes the time step at 9.375e-6 (internal units). Cooling is
off (`GrackleCooling:disable_cooling_for_debugging: 1`), so that the maps show
the light only. The light is deliberately slow: its speed is set by the time
step, not by c. A packet (the light that a particle hands on to its
neighbours) makes one hop of about one smoothing length per step, which is
about 20 km/s here. `GEARFeedback:ISRF_packet_light_speed` keeps its default
(`cap`, 0.99 c). The run lasts a few tens of steps.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-subgrid-radiation=all --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```
`--with-chealpix` is required by the ISRF transport. The number of reception
slots (the direction cells in which a gas particle stores the light it
receives) is the default of `--with-isrf-packet-slots` (12).

The example needs a radiation table whose `Data/Radiation` group carries
`MeanPhotonEnergyPE` and `Integrated_MeanPhotonEnergyPE`, besides the datasets
of the ISRF (section "The Data/Radiation group" of the stellar evolution
table documentation). The pychem table generator produces such a table from
its parameter file `params/chimieparam_radiation_parsec_spectral_popII.py`
(see the section "Where each Radiation field comes from" of the pychem page
"How to generate a Chemistry Table for GEAR"). The published table that
`getRadiationTable.sh` downloads predates the two datasets, so no download is
provided. Pass the file with `radiation_table=/path/to/table.hdf5 ./run.sh`;
the default is
`PopII_parsec_spectral_with_pe_photon_energy.hdf5` in this directory.

# Run
```
clump=0 ./run.sh
clump=1 ./run.sh
```

`run.sh` builds the initial conditions and runs swift in its own directory,
`run_clump<clump>`, which it empties first. Override a setting with an environment
variable: `n_threads`, `gas_density` (atom/cm^3), `gas_mass` (Msun),
`star_mass` (Msun), `clump` (1 or 0), `n_steps` (number of time steps), `swift_exe`, `run_dir`, `run_analysis`
(0 skips the plot) and `extra_args` (further swift arguments).

# Plot
`run.sh` calls `plotShadow.py` from the run directory. It reads
`geometry.json` and the last snapshot, and writes `shadow.png`: the
kernel-mean PE field times the squared distance to the star, on the gas
within 1.5 smoothing lengths of the plane that holds the star-clump axis, and
the ratio of the field with the clump to the field without it. The ratio
needs both runs; `run_clump1` finds `../run_clump0`, so run `clump=0` first.
If `run_clump0` is missing, the plot has the first panel only and says so. To
re-plot after the second run, run `python3 ../plotShadow.py` inside `run_clump1`.
